Given that you cannot modify the batch script or the Java jar file, the solution must rely on MSBuild’s ability to determine whether a file’s content has truly changed, even if its timestamp has been updated. Unfortunately, MSBuild by default uses timestamps to decide whether a file needs rebuilding. To avoid unnecessary rebuilds, you can introduce an additional step in the MSBuild process to check file content.

Here’s how you can implement this within MSBuild:

1. Use a Pre-Build Step to Compare Content

Before invoking the compiler, add a custom MSBuild step to compare the content of the generated files with their previous versions. This step can use a hashing mechanism (e.g., MD5 or SHA256) to detect content changes.

Steps:
	1.	Store a hash (or checksum) of each file in a temporary file during a previous build.
	2.	During subsequent builds, compare the stored hash with the current hash of the file.
	3.	Skip files that have the same hash by preventing MSBuild from considering them as updated.

Add this logic to your .vcxproj file:

<Target Name="PreCompileCheck" BeforeTargets="ClCompile">
  <ItemGroup>
    <GeneratedFiles Include="path\to\generated\*.cpp" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Exec Command="python hash_compare.py %(GeneratedFiles.FullPath)" />
</Target>

The hash_compare.py script can compare content and skip updates for unchanged files. For example:

import hashlib
import os
import sys

def calculate_hash(file_path):
    with open(file_path, 'rb') as f:
        return hashlib.md5(f.read()).hexdigest()

def main():
    for file_path in sys.argv[1:]:
        hash_file = file_path + ".hash"
        current_hash = calculate_hash(file_path)

        if os.path.exists(hash_file):
            with open(hash_file, 'r') as f:
                previous_hash = f.read()
            if previous_hash == current_hash:
                print(f"No changes: {file_path}")
                continue

        with open(hash_file, 'w') as f:
            f.write(current_hash)
        print(f"Updated: {file_path}")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    main()

2. Add a Custom Task to MSBuild

If you prefer to avoid external scripts, you can create a custom MSBuild task to perform the same functionality.

Add the following to your project file:

<Target Name="SkipUnchangedGeneratedFiles" BeforeTargets="ClCompile">
  <ItemGroup>
    <GeneratedFiles Include="path\to\generated\*.cpp" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <Message Text="Checking content of generated files..." />
  <Exec Command="fc /B %(GeneratedFiles.FullPath) %(GeneratedFiles.FullPath).lastbuild" Condition="Exists('%(GeneratedFiles.FullPath).lastbuild')" />
  <Exec Command="copy %(GeneratedFiles.FullPath) %(GeneratedFiles.FullPath).lastbuild" Condition="!Exists('%(GeneratedFiles.FullPath).lastbuild') OR ErrorLevel == 1" />
</Target>

Here:
	•	fc /B compares the binary content of the current file with the stored version.
	•	Only files with differences are updated.

3. Force MSBuild to Skip Unchanged Files

Ensure that MSBuild respects the unchanged files by leveraging the Inputs and Outputs properties. Update your .vcxproj file to include:

<CustomBuild Include="path\to\generated\*.cpp">
  <Command>echo Generating %(Identity)</Command>
  <Outputs>%(FullPath).obj</Outputs>
  <Inputs>%(FullPath)</Inputs>
</CustomBuild>

4. Verify Incremental Build Behavior

After adding the above steps, verify MSBuild’s incremental build behavior:
	1.	Run the build twice consecutively.
	2.	Observe that unchanged files are not rebuilt during the second build.

Use diagnostic logging to confirm:

msbuild project.sln /verbosity:diagnostic > build_log.txt

This approach ensures that only files with content changes trigger a rebuild, even if the batch script updates the timestamps.